* text=auto

# Keep files launched directly by the native Windows shell in its conventional form.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.sh text eol=lf

# Migrations are compared byte-for-byte against the TypeScript constants that
# are their canon (src/schemaSql.ts and friends), and those constants use LF.
# Under plain `text=auto` a Windows checkout rewrote these to CRLF, so every
# such contract test failed there on line endings alone -- measured: the file
# and the constant differed only by 173 CR bytes, and normalising made them
# equal. 23 test files compare SQL this way.
*.sql text eol=lf

# The contract tests slice Rust source between `\n`-anchored markers to check
# that a specific function still carries a specific guard. A CRLF checkout makes
# every such marker miss, the slice comes back empty, and the test reports a
# missing guard that is actually present -- which on Windows hit the very tests
# that exist to protect the Windows launch path. Rust tooling is LF throughout
# (rustfmt emits LF), so nothing else is affected.
*.rs text eol=lf

# Workflow files are read by GitHub's Linux runners and are matched here with
# `\n`-anchored patterns (`/^on:\n.../m`). A CRLF checkout breaks the match, so
# the Windows release-workflow contract could not be checked on Windows.
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# The same `\n`-anchored source slicing protects the TypeScript contracts, and
# TS/TSX is where almost all of it lives: 88 test files slice `api-server.ts`,
# `src/App.tsx` and friends between literal-newline markers to assert a guard is
# still in place. Under plain `text=auto` a Windows checkout rewrote 1220 `.ts`
# and 112 `.tsx` files to CRLF, every such marker missed, and the test reported a
# missing guard that was present -- measured on api-server.ts, whose committed
# blob is LF while the checkout was CRLF. Bun, tsc and Vite all emit and accept
# LF, so nothing else is affected.
*.ts text eol=lf
*.tsx text eol=lf
*.mjs text eol=lf
